	PROCEDURE loademl		&& Loads an email from a .eml file. (EML files contain the MIME source of an email.) Returns a null reference on failure. Note: MHT files can be loaded into an email object by calling this method. Returns Nothing on failure
		*==============================================================================
		* Method:            LoadEML
		* Purpose:           Loads an email from a .eml file.
		* Author:            Bill Anderson
		* Notice:            Copyright (c) 2022 - 2026 The Anderson Files LLC, All Rights Reserved.
		* Parameters:        tcString, the EML file name.
		* Returns:           Chilkat Email Object, or NULL
		* Date Added:        01/18/2022
		*==============================================================================
		
		**
		** Loads an email from a .eml file. (EML files contain the MIME source of an email.) 
		** Returns a null reference on failure.
		**
		** Note: MHT files can be loaded into an email object by calling this method.
		**
		** Update to Version 11.0.0: Method decremented.
		**
		** Returns Nothing on failure
		**
		
		**
		** MHT = EML
		** This is a very important bit of information to know:
		** .eml files are identical to .mht files. The only difference is the filename extension.
		**
		** Both simply contain MIME text. You can save (almost) any web page from Internet Explorer 
		** as a .mht (web archive), rename it by giving it a .eml extension, and then double-click on it. 
		** You’ll notice that the default application for .eml is Outlook Express. 
		** It will display the HTML "email", but with no From, To, CC, or Subject. 
		** You may then add these and send the email.
		
		** With Chilkat, if you already have a .mht file, you may load it into an email object 
		** by calling Chilkat.Email.LoadEml (or Chilkat.Email.SetFromMimeText), 
		** then set the Subject & From properties, 
		** and then call AddTo/AddCC/AddBcc for each recipient, and send.
		** 
		** However, there is one important distinction: HTML emails really shouldn’t contain Javascript. 
		** Email clients flag Javascript as a security threat. 
		** Also, images that are not embedded may be a problem. 
		** When Internet Explorer creates the MHT, offsite images are not embedded. 
		** A typical trick of spammers is to use an image URL 
		** such that it identifies the recipient of the email. 
		** If the HTTP request for the image arrives at their web server 
		** (via the loading and displaying of the email in the email client, such as Outlook), 
		** then they know that the email was received and that the email address is good. 
		** Advance email clients, such as Mozilla’s Thunderbird, 
		** will automatically block the loading of these images. 
		** If you are sending an HTML email, perhaps a newsletter, then you want two things:
		**
		** 1) All images to be embedded.
		** 2) No Javascript
		**
		** The Chilkat MHT component takes care of #1 as best as possible — assuming the images 
		** can be downloaded and embedded in the email at the time of the function call. 
		** Chilkat MHT (in the calls that return EML) strips Javascript 
		** and selects the "noscript" option from within the HTML if it is present.
		**
		
		LPARAMETERS tcString AS Character
		
		LOCAL loChilkatObject AS CHILKAT_MAILMAN_COM_CALL, ;
		loException AS Exception, loChilkatEmail AS CHILKAT_EMAIL_COM_CALL
		
		STORE NULL TO m.loChilkatObject, m.loException, m.loChilkatEmail
		
		WITH THIS
		
		  loChilkatObject = .oChilkat
		
		  ** Have a good object test
		  IF VARTYPE(m.loChilkatObject) == T_OBJECT ;
		  AND (NOT ISNULL(m.loChilkatObject))
		
		    TRY
		
		      loChilkatEmail = m.loChilkatObject.LoadEML(m.tcString)
		
		    CATCH TO m.loException
		
		      .CallToChilkatError(m.loException, THIS)
		
		    ENDTRY
		
		  ELSE
		
		    .MissingObjectError(THIS)
		
		  ENDIF VARTYPE(m.loChilkatObject) == T_OBJECT ;
		  AND (NOT ISNULL(m.loChilkatObject))
		  ** End have a good object test
		
		ENDWITH
		
		STORE NULL TO m.loChilkatObject, m.loException
		
		RETURN m.loChilkatEmail
		
		** Design time IntelliSense test
		#IF .F.
		
		  loChilkatObject = NEWOBJECT(CHILKAT_MAILMAN_COM_CALL)
		  loChilkatEmail = NEWOBJECT(CHILKAT_EMAIL_COM_CALL)
		  loException = NEWOBJECT([Exception])
		
		#ENDIF .F.
