	PROCEDURE copyfileattr		&& Sets the date/time and other attributes of a remote file to be equal to that of a local file.
		*==============================================================================
		* Method:            CopyFileAttr
		* Purpose:           Sets the date/time and other attributes 
		*                    of a remote file to be equal to that of a local file.
		* Author:            Bill Anderson
		* Notice:            Copyright (c) 2022 - 2026 The Anderson Files LLC, All Rights Reserved.
		* Parameters:        tcLocalFileName -- the local file name to get attributes
		*                    tcRemoteFileName, the remote file to receive attributes
		*                    tuIsHandle -- determines if local file is name or handle
		* Returns:           Returns 1 for success, 0 for failure.
		* Date Added:        04/20/2022
		*==============================================================================
		
		**
		** Sets the date/time and other attributes of a remote file to be equal to that of a local file.
		** 
		** The attributes copied depend on the SFTP version of the server:
		** 
		** <b>SFTP v3 (and below)</b>
		**     Last-Modified Date/Time
		**     Last-Access Date/Time
		** 
		** <b>SFTP v4, v5</b>
		**     Last-Modified Date/Time
		**     Last-Access Date/Time
		**     Create Date/Time
		** 
		** <b>SFTP v6 (and above)</b>
		**     Last-Modified Date/Time
		**     Last-Access Date/Time
		**     Create Date/Time
		**     Read-Only Flag
		**     Hidden Flag
		**     Archive Flag
		**     Compressed Flag
		**     Encrypted Flag
		** 
		** Notes:
		** 
		** (1) The Last-Access date/time may or may not be set. 
		** Chilkat has found that the Last-Access time is set to the current date/time, 
		** which is probably a result of the operating system setting it based 
		** on when the SFTP server is touching the file.
		** 
		** (2) At the time of this writing, it is unknown whether the compressed/encryption settings 
		** for a local file are transferred to the remote file. 
		** For example, does the remote file become compressed 
		** and/or encrypted just by setting the flags? 
		** It may depend on the SFTP server and/or the remote filesystem.
		** 
		** (3) Dates/times are sent in GMT. SFTP servers should convert GMT times to local time zones.
		** Returns 1 for success, 0 for failure.
		**
		
		LPARAMETERS tcLocalFileName AS Character, ;
		tcRemoteFileName AS Character, tuIsHandle AS Unknown
		
		LOCAL loChilkatObject AS CHILKAT_SFTP_COM_CALL, ;
		loException AS Exception, liReturn AS Integer, ;
		luReturn AS Unknown, liIsHandle AS Integer
		
		STORE 0 TO m.liReturn, m.luReturn, m.liIsHandle
		STORE NULL TO m.loChilkatObject, m.loException
		
		WITH THIS
		
		  loChilkatObject = .oChilkat
		
		  ** Have a good object test
		  IF VARTYPE(m.loChilkatObject) == T_OBJECT ;
		  AND (NOT ISNULL(m.loChilkatObject))
		
		    liIsHandle = .LogicalToBit(m.tuIsHandle)
		
		    TRY
		
		      STORE m.loChilkatObject.CopyFileAttr(m.tcLocalFileName, m.tcRemoteFileName, m.liIsHandle) ;
		      TO m.liReturn, m.luReturn
		
		    CATCH TO m.loException
		
		      .CallToChilkatError(m.loException, THIS)
		
		    ENDTRY
		
		  ELSE
		
		    .MissingObjectError(THIS)
		
		  ENDIF VARTYPE(m.loChilkatObject) == T_OBJECT ;
		  AND (NOT ISNULL(m.loChilkatObject))
		  ** End have a good object test
		
		  luReturn = .BitToLogical(m.liReturn)
		
		ENDWITH
		
		STORE NULL TO m.loChilkatObject, m.loException
		
		RETURN m.luReturn
		
		** Design time IntelliSense test
		#IF .F.
		
		  loChilkatObject = NEWOBJECT(CHILKAT_SFTP_COM_CALL)
		  loException = NEWOBJECT([Exception])
		
		#ENDIF .F.
		** End design time IntelliSense test
	ENDPROC

