	PROCEDURE connect		&& Establishes a secure SSL/TLS or a plain non-secure TCP connection with a remote host:port. This is a blocking call. The maximum wait time (in milliseconds) is passed in maxWaitMs.
		*==============================================================================
		* Method:            Connect
		* Purpose:           Establishes a secure SSL/TLS or a plain non-secure 
		*                    TCP connection with a remote host:port.
		* Author:            Bill Anderson
		* Notice:            Copyright (c) 2022 - 2026 The Anderson Files LLC, All Rights Reserved.
		* Parameters:        tcHostName -- the host name.
		*                    tiPort -- the port to use.
		*                    tuSSL -- whether or not SSL/TLS is used.
		*                    tiMaxWaitMs -- the maximum millisecond wait
		* Returns:           Returns 1 for success, 0 for failure.
		* Date Added:        05/28/2022
		*==============================================================================
		
		**
		** Establishes a secure SSL/TLS or a plain non-secure TCP connection with a remote host:port. 
		** This is a blocking call. The maximum wait time (in milliseconds) is passed in maxWaitMs. 
		** This is the amount of time the app is willing to wait for the TCP connection to be accepted.
		** 
		** To establish an SSL/TLS connection, set ssl = 1, 
		** otherwise set ssl = 0 for a normal TCP connection. 
		** Note: The timeouts that apply to the internal SSL/TLS handshaking messages 
		** are the MaxReadIdleMs and MaxSendIdleMs properties.
		** 
		** Note: Connections do not automatically close because of inactivity. 
		** A connection will remain open indefinitely even if there is no activity.
		** 
		** Important: All TCP-based Internet communications, regardless of the protocol 
		** (such as HTTP, FTP, SSH, IMAP, POP3, SMTP, etc.), and regardless of SSL/TLS, 
		** begin with establishing a TCP connection to a remote host:port. 
		** External security-related infrastructure such as software firewalls (Windows Firewall), 
		** hardware firewalls, anti-virus, at either source or destination (or both) can block the connection. 
		** If the connection fails, make sure to check all potential external causes of blockage.
		** 
		** Question: How do I Choose the TLS version, such as 1.2? 
		** Answer: The client does not specifically choose the TLS version. 
		** In the TLS handshake (which is what occurs internally in this method), 
		** the client tells the server the version of the TLS protocol it wishes to use, 
		** which should be the highest version is supports. 
		** In this case, (at the time of this writing on 22-June-2017) it is TLS 1.2. 
		** The server then chooses the TLS version that will actually be used. 
		** In most cases it will be TLS 1.2. The client can then choose to accept 
		** or reject the connection based on the TLS version chosen by the server. 
		** By default, Chilkat will reject anything lower than SSL 3.0 (i.e. SSL 2.0 or lower is rejected). 
		** The SslProtocol property can be set to change what is accepted by Chilkat. 
		** For example, it can be set to "TLS 1.0 or higher".
		**
		** Fixed for Version 51: The maxWaitMs argument was being ignored 
		** and the method would always wait a max of 30 seconds. This was fixed.
		** 
		** Fixed for Version 59: The server would close the TLS connection 
		** during initial handshake (after receiving the ClientHello) 
		** if it picked the cipher suite TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA in TLS 1.2. 
		**
		** Fixed in version 99: In some cases, Chilkat failed to establish connections 
		** trying to use aes-gcm. This was fixed. (It was not common.)
		** 
		** Returns 1 for success, 0 for failure.
		**
		
		LPARAMETERS tcHostName AS Character, ;
		tiPort AS Integer, tuSSL AS Unknown, tiMaxWaitMs AS Integer
		
		LOCAL loChilkatObject AS CHILKAT_SOCKET_COM_CALL, liSSL AS Integer, ;
		loException AS Exception, liReturn AS Integer, luReturn AS Unknown
		
		STORE 0 TO m.liReturn, m.luReturn, m.liSSL 
		STORE NULL TO m.loChilkatObject, m.loException
		
		WITH THIS
		
		  loChilkatObject = .oChilkat
		
		  ** Have a good object test
		  IF VARTYPE(m.loChilkatObject) == T_OBJECT ;
		  AND (NOT ISNULL(m.loChilkatObject))
		
		    liSSL = .LogicalToBit(m.liSSL)
		
		    TRY
		
		      STORE m.loChilkatObject.Connect(m.tcHostName, m.tiPort, m.liSSL, m.tiMaxWaitMs) ;
		      TO m.liReturn, m.luReturn
		
		    CATCH TO m.loException
		
		      .CallToChilkatError(m.loException, THIS)
		
		    ENDTRY
		
		  ELSE
		
		    .MissingObjectError(THIS)
		
		  ENDIF VARTYPE(m.loChilkatObject) == T_OBJECT ;
		  AND (NOT ISNULL(m.loChilkatObject))
		  ** End have a good object test
		
		  luReturn = .BitToLogical(m.liReturn)
		
		ENDWITH
		
		STORE NULL TO m.loChilkatObject, m.loException
		
		RETURN m.luReturn
		
		** Design time IntelliSense test
		#IF .F.
		
		  loChilkatObject = NEWOBJECT(CHILKAT_SOCKET_COM_CALL)
		  loException = NEWOBJECT([Exception])
		
		#ENDIF .F.
		** End design time IntelliSense test
	ENDPROC

